 ## Background
-The concept of using lookup tables for isosurface extraction originated with Lorensen and Cline in 1987, who introduced the Marching Cubes algorithm and the standard 256-case lookup table [S8, unofficial]. The table structure, consisting of an edge intersection table and a triangle connection table, became the de facto standard for efficient mesh generation from volumetric data [S8, unofficial]. Subsequent research has focused on optimizing these tables, such as using symmetry mapping to reduce storage [S1], or modifying them to resolve topological ambiguities [S7, S8, unofficial]. The method is widely used in medical imaging, scientific visualization, and computer graphics for surface reconstruction from CT, MRI, and other volumetric datasets [S1][S2, unofficial].
+The method relies on precomputed tables to avoid runtime branching [G]. Automatic construction of these tables can be performed by detecting patch boundaries as groups of rings of linear contours on cell faces [S1]. Variants such as Bloomenthal polygonization generalize this approach for algebraic surfaces [S2, unofficial].
 
